---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import catalog from '../../data/generated/knowledge-catalog.json';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, '');
const docs = catalog.documents;
const domainDetails = [
  { name: 'Foundations', key: 'foundations', text:'The shared ideas that make technical systems understandable: logic, models, information, computation, scientific reasoning, security principles, and the methods of operational work.' },
  { name: 'Network Knowledge', key: 'network', text:'How devices and systems communicate, from topology and infrastructure to protocols, addressing, services, routing, and the operation of networks.' },
  { name: 'Endpoint Knowledge', key: 'endpoint', text:'What happens inside computing devices: execution, resources, storage, identity enforcement, connectivity, and the functions operating systems provide.' },
  { name: 'Application Knowledge', key: 'application', text:'The mechanisms that applications use to process information, expose services, manage state, interact with people, and execute work.' },
  { name: 'Cloud Knowledge', key: 'cloud', text:'The functions delivered through cloud platforms, including compute, storage, networking, identity, integration, observability, and governance.' },
  { name: 'Industrial Knowledge', key: 'industrial', text:'The systems and relationships that connect physical processes, sensing, control, communications, automation, and safety.' },
  { name: 'IoT Knowledge', key: 'iot', text:'The roles played by connected devices, from sensing and actuation through gateways, coordination, edge computing, and maintenance.' },
  { name: 'Identity Knowledge', key: 'identity', text:'How identities are established, authenticated, authorized, governed, and trusted across technical environments.' },
];
const domains = domainDetails.map(domain => ({
  ...domain,
  index: docs.find(doc => doc.file === domain.key + '/README.md'),
  count: docs.filter(doc => doc.domain === domain.key).length,
}));
const urlFor = (doc: { slug: string }) => base + '/knowledge/read/' + doc.slug + '/';
const overview = docs.find(doc => doc.file === 'README.md');
---
<BaseLayout title="Knowledge" description="A browsable technical knowledge library based on foundational concepts, seven specialized domains, and explicit links between source documents.">
  <header class="page-heading">
    <p class="eyebrow">SECURITY KNOWLEDGE</p>
    <h1>Knowledge</h1>
    <p class="lead">I organize knowledge so that individual facts can become part of a larger, more useful understanding.</p>
  </header>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="knowledge-reason">
    <div class="body-copy narrative-copy">
      <h2 id="knowledge-reason">Why I built this library.</h2>
      <p>One of the problems I keep returning to is how easily technical knowledge becomes fragmented. We learn a command in one place, a protocol in another, and a security concept somewhere else. Each piece may be accurate on its own, yet the relationships between them are often left unexplained.</p>
      <p>I wanted a reference organized around those relationships. Instead of beginning with a vendor or product, I begin with the concepts and functions that make systems work. Specific operating systems, cloud providers, applications, and technologies can then be examined as implementations of those more general ideas.</p>
      <p>The result is an evolving technical knowledge repository with <strong>Foundations</strong> and seven specialized domains. It is intended to support exploration rather than impose a mandatory course sequence. Someone may start with an unfamiliar network function, follow a link back to a general concept in Foundations, and then move to an implementation in another domain.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="knowledge-architecture">
    <h2 id="knowledge-architecture">A hierarchy for navigation. Relationships for understanding.</h2>
    <div class="body-copy narrative-copy">
      <p>Within the specialized domains, the primary navigation model is <strong>Domain → Function → Implementation</strong>. The hierarchy helps someone find a subject without guessing which product or company should own it. Alternate views, such as operating-system families or cloud providers, provide other ways to discover the same canonical material.</p>
      <p>But a directory tree is not a complete explanation of knowledge. An idea can depend on another concept, appear in several systems, or be implemented through different mechanisms. The library's internal Markdown links begin to expose those connections without treating every folder as a strict conceptual parent.</p>
      <p>At this stage, the interactive link explorer shows actual links between documents. It is not yet a formal semantic knowledge graph with approved relation predicates. I prefer that limitation to be visible rather than suggesting precision that the current data does not support.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="resource-links">
      <a class="primary-link" href={base + '/knowledge/start-here/'}>Start with a reading path</a>
      <a class="secondary-link" href={base + '/knowledge/browse/'}>Browse and search the library</a>
      <a class="secondary-link" href={base + '/knowledge/graph/'}>Explore document links</a>
      {overview && <a class="secondary-link" href={urlFor(overview)}>Read the repository overview</a>}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="guided-paths-title">
    <div class="narrative-panel">
      <p class="eyebrow">AN INVITATION TO EXPLORE</p>
      <h2 id="guided-paths-title">Not sure where to begin?</h2>
      <p>I find it easier to learn a technical subject when I have a question worth following. I've written three short introductions that connect existing references: how observations become evidence for a claim, how we model the behavior of systems, and how abstract logic relates to implemented mechanisms.</p>
      <p>They aren't courses or a prescribed reading order. They're ways into the library, intended to make the connections between individual subjects more visible while preserving the source documents' editorial status and limitations.</p>
      <a class="text-link" href={base + '/knowledge/start-here/'}>Choose a reading path ↗</a>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="domains-title">
    <div class="section-intro">
      <div><p class="eyebrow">THE KNOWLEDGE MAP</p><h2 id="domains-title">Start anywhere. Follow the connections.</h2></div>
      <p>Foundations provides shared concepts. The other domains examine how those concepts appear in different classes of technical systems.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card-grid">
      {domains.map(domain => (
        <article class="card">
          <p class="status">{domain.count} source documents</p>
          <h3>{domain.index ? <a href={urlFor(domain.index)}>{domain.name}</a> : domain.name}</h3>
          <p>{domain.text}</p>
          {domain.index && <a class="text-link" href={urlFor(domain.index)}>Explore this area ↗</a>}
        </article>
      ))}
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section" aria-labelledby="knowledge-status-heading">
    <div class="narrative-panel">
      <p class="eyebrow">AN EVOLVING REFERENCE</p>
      <h2 id="knowledge-status-heading">An organized subject isn't necessarily a finished one.</h2>
      <p>Some pages are substantive technical references. Others are approved subject inventories, working architectural decisions, or material still awaiting review. I want those stages to remain distinguishable. A document appearing in the library does not automatically mean that its technical claims have received independent expert review or final publication approval.</p>
      <p>This website is a reading interface for the public <a href={'https://github.com/' + catalog.source} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Security-Education repository</a>. The source remains authoritative, and each document links back to the exact repository revision used to build the site. The current build includes {catalog.count} Markdown documents from revision <code>{catalog.revision.slice(0, 12)}</code>.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
